{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_e5nmmc33mr5polqke43g3zvmst22bbjxudjq2s76f4ovpcmc6k5a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four phases are complete. Summary:\n\n## Phase A — both build and run\n- C: `cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .` → `c_src/build/driver`\n- Rust: `cd translation && cargo build --release` → `translation/target/release/driver` (compiled clean, no errors or warnings)\n\n## Phase B/C — one real mismatch found, in a large and reachable part of the input space\n\n`compact_runs()` rewrites each run as a 2-byte `value,count` pair. With `flags & 0x02` and `param1 == 1` **every** run compacts, so a 256-byte input grows to as much as 512 bytes — but `main.c` only reserves `uint8_t buffer[256]`. The C writes up to 256 bytes past its own array, into the rest of `main`'s frame, and the result is observable and fully deterministic.\n\n`objdump -d` gave the exact layout (`buffer` at `rbp-0x130`, other locals above it):\n\n| bytes past `buffer` | slot | observable effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| 256–280 | `length`, `param1/2`, `flags` | dead after the call — invisible |\n| 280–288 | `size_t new_length` | rewritten by `main`, so it prints as the LE image of `new_length` |\n| 288–296 | print-loop `size_t i` | prints as the loop counter's own current value |\n| 296–312 | read-loop `i`, saved `rbp` | harmless |\n| 312+ | return address | clobbered ⇒ `main` dies on `ret` by **SIGSEGV**, stdout lost (buffered pipe, < BUFSIZ) |\n\nThe old Rust printed the clean 4096-byte result and exited 0 in all of these. Examples: `2 1 0 141 …` → C `… 280 26 1`, Rust `… 280 1 1`; `2 1 0 256 …` → C empty output + SIGSEGV, Rust a 512-byte line + exit 0.\n\nFixes, in the Rust only:\n- `src/lib.rs` — all stores route through a `Buffer` wrapper recording the highest index written.\n- `src/main.rs` — models that frame: reaching index ≥ 312 resets `SIGSEGV` to `SIG_DFL` and `raise()`s it without flushing; otherwise bytes 280–296 print as the aliased locals.\n\nA subtlety worth flagging: the trigger is the *highest index ever written*, not the final length. Singles followed by one long run grow past 312 and shrink back — `new_len == 202` yet the C still dies. That case is tested.\n\n## Phase D — gate\n- 30 tests in `translation/tests/differential.rs`, driving **both binaries as subprocesses** and comparing stdout, stderr and exit status (including death by signal) — no library calls, no `#[ignore]`, nothing skipped.\n- Passes in debug and release (~50s; the suite disables core dumps in the children, identically for both, because each SIGSEGV otherwise costs ~0.3s to `systemd-coredump`).\n- ~12,500 additional randomly generated inputs compared with zero differences.\n- `c_src/` md5sums unchanged; only the `build/` directory was added.\n- `translation/ERRORS.md` records the mismatch, the C quirks deliberately reproduced (scanf crossing newlines, `%u` accepting signs and wrapping, saturating conversions, partial-token failures shifting the error to the *next* field, shared `param1`), the statically unreachable C branches, and the one assumption: the frame model matches the documented unoptimised build.\n\nOne caveat you should be aware of: the crash/aliasing behaviour is reproduced against gcc at `-O0`, which is what the documented `cmake ..` build produces. If the C were rebuilt optimised, `new_length` and `i` would live in registers and the out-of-bounds writes would alias something else — that is a genuinely different program. All behaviour for inputs whose compacted length stays within 256 bytes is independent of this."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2149,"cache_read_input_tokens":159324,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2149,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff993cf9-42c6-4084-af5f-96944f4fc84f","uuid":"37eda818-40b8-47ca-8af8-fdf81f67ddf2","timestamp":"2026-08-28T05:00:45.984Z"}
